<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Purandara Dasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1484–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Purandara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dasa">Dasa</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet-saint and composer of the Haridasa devotional movement who lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. A wealthy merchant before he gave away his property, he composed thousands of devotional songs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. He systematised the teaching of Carnatic music, devising graded exercises still used by beginners, and is traditionally called the father of Carnatic music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/purandara-dasa.jpg" alt="Purandara Dasa" width="230" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Music and dance on stamps · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:481_Purandardasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Purandara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dasa">Dasa</a> was born into a Kannada Deshastha Madhva Brahmin family; his father was a diamond merchant, and he himself traded in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and jewellery before giving his wealth away to become a Haridasa, a servant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. His birthplace has long been disputed. Some placed it at Purandaraghatta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> or at Purandaragad near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, the latter now regarded as a mistake that ties his pen name to a military camp. In 2018 a five-member committee set up by the Government of Karnataka reported that Tirthahalli was the likeliest place, at Araga in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvaita-vedanta">Dvaita</a> philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhvacharya</a> and took his Haridasa initiation from Vyasatirtha at Hampi. Manuscripts written by Tallapaka Chinanna, grandson of the poet Annamacharya, record that the two met at Tirumala and sang devotional songs together before Sri Venkateshwara. His songs, in the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada language</a>, carried the teachings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> to ordinary people and often criticised the faults of society. In one, comparing cows of different colours and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> of different shapes, he argued that birth alone cannot decide whether a person is high or low. His son Madhvapati gave 1564 as the year of his death; the year of his birth is uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Musicologists call him the Sangeeta Pitamaha, or grandsire, of Carnatic music. He made the raga Mayamalavagowla the basic scale for teaching and arranged graded lessons, among them swaravalis, janti swaras, alankaras, lakshana geetas, prabandhas, ugabhogas, sooladis and kritis. He set his words to the ragas of his day, brought folk ragas into the mainstream and used colloquial language. His sooladis are regarded as the standard for raga lakshana, and scholars credit him with standardising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> mettus. A legend holds him to be an incarnation of Narada. India issued a stamp in his honour on 14 January 1964, the 400th anniversary of his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Purandara Dasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The composer <b>Purandara Dasa</b>, traditionally regarded as the father of Carnatic music, lived and worked within the empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Vijayanagara Empire the Haridasas, among them <b>Purandara Dasa</b>, sang to Vishnu in Kannada.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thirthahalli, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Hampi, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.7,75.23&amp;year=1536" aria-label="Thirthahalli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Purandara Dasa"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3786 3839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3786 3839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.7,75.23&amp;year=1536">What was here in 1536? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
